-The prototype integrates with continuous glucose monitors (CGMs) to read real-time glucose data and trigger insulin delivery automatically, a step toward a closed-loop artificial pancreas system. The core engineering challenge was building a delivery mechanism that's reliable, miniaturized, and safe enough to trust with medication.
+InsuSense started with one question: what if the system could close the loop?
 
-This project required going deep on both the biology and the engineering simultaneously. I had to understand how glucose-insulin dynamics work (lag times, correction factors, and carb ratios) before I could design a system that wouldn't over- or under-dose.
+## The prototype
 
-The research phase alone was one of the most intensive I've done. The clinical constraints on any device that touches medication delivery are strict for good reason. This project gave me enormous respect for the people who build medical devices professionally.
+It connects to continuous glucose monitors (CGMs), reads real-time glucose data, and triggers insulin delivery automatically. That's a step toward a closed-loop artificial pancreas.
 
+The hard engineering part: a delivery mechanism that's reliable, miniaturized, and safe enough to trust with medication.
+
+## What it took
+
+I had to learn the biology and the engineering at the same time. Before I could design anything that wouldn't over- or under-dose, I had to understand how glucose and insulin behave: lag times, correction factors, and carb ratios.
+
+The research was one of the most intensive I've done. The rules for any device that delivers medication are strict for good reason. It gave me a lot of respect for the people who build medical devices professionally.
+
 ---
 
 **Outcome:** Designed a non-invasive insulin delivery prototype that reads real-time glucose data from a CGM and automates dosing, a step toward a closed-loop artificial pancreas.
 
 **What I took from it:**
-- Closed-loop medical systems require understanding the biology and the engineering simultaneously. You can't hand one off to someone else, because the lag times, correction factors, and dosing logic are all connected.
-- Clinical constraints on medication delivery devices exist for good reason. Designing around them early is what separates a concept that could ship from one that can't.
-- This was the most intensive research I've done. It also gave me the most respect for healthcare engineering as a discipline: the stakes make every design decision heavier.
